---
import PhaseLayout from '../../layouts/PhaseLayout.astro';
---

<PhaseLayout title="Computer Science Phase 2 — Polymath.Sys" activeNav="courses">

<style>
  .cpn { display:flex; gap:0; margin-bottom:2.5rem; border:1px solid #222; width:fit-content; }
  .cpn-link { font-family:monospace; font-size:11px; letter-spacing:0.08em; text-transform:uppercase; padding:0.45rem 1.1rem; color:#555; text-decoration:none; border-right:1px solid #222; }
  .cpn-link:last-child { border-right:none; }
  .cpn-link.active { background:#111; color:#fff; }
  .cpn-link:hover:not(.active) { background:#f3f3f3; color:#111; }
  .cpn-back { display:inline-block; font-family:monospace; font-size:11px; color:#888; text-decoration:none; margin-bottom:1.2rem; letter-spacing:0.05em; }
  .cpn-back:hover { color:#111; }
</style>

  <a href="/courses/cs" class="cpn-back">&larr; Computer Science Overview</a>
  <div class="cpn">
    <a href="/courses/cs-phase-1" class="cpn-link">Phase 1</a>
    <a href="/courses/cs-phase-2" class="cpn-link active">Phase 2</a>
    <a href="/courses/cs-phase-3" class="cpn-link">Phase 3</a>
  </div>

  <div class="track-header">
    <div class="track-badge badge-dsa">Phase 2 &middot; Months 5-9 &middot; ~95h</div>
    <div class="track-title">Phase 2 &mdash; Networks &amp; Databases</div>
    <div class="track-sub">Learn how data moves across systems and how it persists at scale. Networks gives you the protocols that underpin every distributed application; databases gives you the storage engines, query optimizers, and transaction models that every production system depends on.</div>
  </div>

    <!-- CS Step 3: Computer Networks -->
    <div class="step" id="cs2">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('cs2')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">3</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Computer Networks &mdash; TCP/IP, HTTP, sockets, protocols</div><div class="step-meta">Months 5-7 &middot; ~45 hours</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-cs2">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-cs2">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://gaia.cs.umass.edu/kurose_ross/online_lectures.htm" target="_blank" class="res-link">Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach &mdash; Kurose &amp; Ross <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The standard networking textbook used by most universities. Starts from the application layer (HTTP, DNS, SMTP) and works down to the physical layer. The Wireshark labs let you capture and analyze real network traffic. Free lecture videos and supplementary material on the authors' website.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Standard textbook</span><span class="rtag book">Book</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@PowerCertAnimatedVideos" target="_blank" class="res-link">PowerCert Animated Videos &mdash; Networking Fundamentals (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Clear animated explanations of networking concepts: OSI model, TCP vs UDP, DNS, DHCP, NAT, subnetting, VLANs. Each video is 5-15 minutes. Watch before reading the corresponding Kurose chapter to build intuition first.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M14.7 6.3a1 1 0 0 0 0 1.4l1.6 1.6a1 1 0 0 0 1.4 0l3.77-3.77a6 6 0 0 1-7.94 7.94l-6.91 6.91a2.12 2.12 0 0 1-3-3l6.91-6.91a6 6 0 0 1 7.94-7.94l-3.76 3.76z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://beej.us/guide/bgnet/" target="_blank" class="res-link">Beej's Guide to Network Programming <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The classic free guide to socket programming in C. Covers TCP/UDP sockets, client-server architecture, multiplexing with select/poll. After reading this, you can build a chat server, an HTTP server, or a simple load balancer from scratch.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Essential for sockets</span><span class="rtag tool">Practice</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLoCMsyE1cvdWKsLVyf6cPwCLDIZnOj0NS" target="_blank" class="res-link">Hussein Nasser &mdash; Networking &amp; Backend Engineering (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Deep dives into TCP internals, HTTP/2, HTTP/3, TLS handshakes, connection pooling, and how real systems handle networking at scale. The practical perspective that textbooks miss. His "TCP is way more complicated than you think" series is exceptional.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>Build to prove mastery:</b> Write an HTTP/1.1 server from scratch in C using sockets. It should handle GET requests, serve static files, support persistent connections, and return proper status codes. Then add multithreading. This single project touches networking, OS concepts, and C programming simultaneously.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- CS Step 4: Databases -->
    <div class="step" id="cs3">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('cs3')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">4</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Databases &mdash; storage engines, query optimization, transactions</div><div class="step-meta">Months 7-9 &middot; ~50 hours</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-cs3">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-cs3">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLSE8ODhjZXjbj8BMuIrRcacnQh20hmY9g" target="_blank" class="res-link">CMU 15-445 &mdash; Database Systems (Andy Pavlo, YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Andy Pavlo's legendary CMU database course, free on YouTube. Covers storage engines, buffer pool management, B+ trees, hash indexes, query processing, query optimization, concurrency control (2PL, MVCC), and crash recovery. The projects have you build a complete database storage engine in C++. Best database course available anywhere.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Best DB course</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">Designing Data-Intensive Applications &mdash; Martin Kleppmann</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The "DDIA" book. Covers data models, storage engines, encoding, replication, partitioning, transactions, consistency, batch processing, and stream processing. This is the bridge between database theory and distributed systems engineering. Every senior engineer has read this book.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Must-read</span><span class="rtag book">Book</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://cstack.github.io/db_tutorial/" target="_blank" class="res-link">Let's Build a Simple Database &mdash; cstack.github.io <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">A free tutorial that walks you through building a SQLite clone in C from scratch. You implement a REPL, SQL compiler, virtual machine, B-tree storage, and persistence. Understanding how a database works internally changes how you write queries forever.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag tool">Build project</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight"><b>The key insight for interviews:</b> Understanding B+ trees, write-ahead logging, and MVCC at the implementation level is what separates senior database engineers from junior ones. When you can explain why PostgreSQL uses MVCC instead of 2PL, and the trade-offs involved, you demonstrate deep systems thinking.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

</PhaseLayout>
